# Credentials. Never commit these; a leaked PyPI or HuggingFace token lets
# anyone publish under this project's name. Tokens belong in ~/.pypirc (chmod
# 600, outside the repository) or a keyring -- note twine does not read .env,
# so putting one there would not even work.
.env
.env.*
.pypirc
*.token
secrets.*

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# OS
*.DS_Store

# Build artifacts
build/*
dist/*
*.egg-info/
# build/* and dist/* match only the top-level directories, because a pattern
# containing a slash is anchored to this file. docs/build is therefore not
# covered by them and is ignored separately below.
#
# dist/ holds the sdist and wheel from `python -m build` (~50 MB, since the
# bundled foundation checkpoints ship inside the wheel) and *.egg-info/ the
# build metadata. Both are regenerated on every build; pyproject.toml and
# setup.py are the sources and stay tracked.

# Trained models and checkpoints
*.pth
test/*/*.pt
test/painn/model_*

# Datasets generated by the test scripts. test/*.traj is a tracked fixture
# and must not be ignored, which is why these are one level deep or dft-prefixed.
test/dft*.traj
test/*/*.traj

# Run outputs and logs
*output*
*.log
*log.txt

# Scratch directories
iann/models/test
test/test

# Agent tool configuration, generated by `iann agent install` 
.claude/

# Ignoring it here keeps the two histories from mixing.
proj/*

# Machine-readable run artifacts, e.g. the run.json manifest that `iann train`
# writes beside a checkpoint.
*.json

# Sphinx output. Regenerated by any build, so it is not tracked -- Read the Docs
# builds from source on its own servers, and test/docs/build.sh writes to
# test/docs/output/ instead.
docs/build

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two patterns above are broader than they look, deliberately left as they are
# because narrowing them would start tracking files that are currently ignored:
#
#   *output*  matches the substring anywhere in a path, so a source file named
#             output_writer.py would be ignored too. Name new sources carefully,
#             or add a negation such as !iann/**/output*.py.
#   *.json    ignores every JSON file in the repository. If a JSON file ever
#             needs to be tracked, force it with `git add -f` or add a negation
#             such as !package.json.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
